options.explanation:
Saying "$y$ is an implicit function of $x$" means the same as saying that $y(x)$ is a function whose equation is unknown.

The derivative of $y(x)$ is $y'(x)$, or $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$.

We treat any expression that contains $y$ as a composite function, so differentiating it involves the chain rule.

For example, this is how we differentiate the term $3y^2$ (from our equation) implicitly:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{d}{dx}(3y^2)&=3\dfrac{d}{dx}[y(x)]^2&&\gray{\text{Write }y\text{ as }y(x)}
\\\\
&=3\cdot 2y(x)\cdot y'(x)&&\gray{\text{Chain rule}}
\\\\
&=6y\cdot\dfrac{dy}{dx}&&\gray{\text{Go back to }y\text{ and }\dfrac{dy}{dx}}
\end{align}$
